# Import of the two SegAlign trees (1.7 GB on disk, ~83% of it one virtualenv and
# one `.venv`). Only what goal 4 argues from is wanted, so the rule is the same
# deny-by-default used by the other two imports: nothing here is tracked unless it
# is named below.
#
# This file lives in `.scratch/py-rudimentary/` rather than the repo root so that
# deleting `.scratch/` at the end of the merge removes the rules along with what
# they describe -- see `.scratch/README.md`.
#
# The repo-root `.gitignore` still applies underneath these rules, and is what
# keeps `.DS_Store`, `__pycache__/`, `*.pyc`, `.venv`, and `.pytest_cache/` out
# even inside the re-included directories. Do not restate those here; a negation
# added below could re-admit them by accident. This matters more in this import
# than in the other two, because `data/` below is re-included by a `**` pattern
# that would otherwise reach into `__pycache__/`.
#
# BOTH renames recorded in `.scratch/README.md` were needed this time, unlike the
# `hmm-lush` import where neither was:
#   SegAlign-Draft/.git -> .git-disabled     embedded repo; `git add` on a path
#   segalign/.git       -> .git-disabled     inside one silently stages nothing
#   segalign/CLAUDE.md  -> CLAUDE.md.orig    would enter pfsmgraph sessions
# Revisions were captured before disabling, and are in `.scratch/README.md`.
#
# `segalign/.claude/settings.local.json` is another project's tool configuration.
# It is not a hazard the way the nested `CLAUDE.md` was -- Claude Code reads
# `.claude/` from the project root, not from arbitrary subdirectories -- but it is
# not ours and the catch-all excludes it.

# --- deny everything at this level -------------------------------------------
/*
!/.gitignore

# Our own writing about the import. This negation is easy to forget and fails
# silently: a file written here without it is simply invisible to `git status`,
# so the work looks committed and is not. It has now caught out two of the three
# imports, so treat it as the default failure mode rather than a one-off.
!/*.md

!/segalign/
!/SegAlign-Draft/

# =============================================================================
# segalign -- the third `dataseq` implementation proper
# =============================================================================
# `ca97809` (2025-09-05), a refactoring of SegAlign-Draft that extracts a `seq/`
# subpackage. This is the tree goal 4 compares against; `src/segalign/seq/` is
# the merge-relevant code and everything else here is either what it imports or
# what tests it.

/segalign/*
!/segalign/README.md

# Declared dependencies. `docs/plan/DEFERRED.md` makes `dataseq`'s own third-party
# dependencies a function of what the merge sources actually need, and this file
# is that evidence: `Dataset` holds `pd.DataFrame`, so unlike the Lush original
# (whose translation is stdlib-only) this implementation cannot be read as
# supporting `dependencies = []`.
!/segalign/pyproject.toml

!/segalign/src/
!/segalign/tests/

/segalign/src/*
!/segalign/src/segalign/

# --- the package: seq/, plus exactly what importing it requires --------------
# `from segalign.seq import Dataset` executes `src/segalign/__init__.py`, which
# does `from . import seq`, `from . import glob` and `from . import data`. So
# `glob/` and `data/` are not optional context: without them the merge target
# does not import, and the tests below do not run. This is the same criterion the
# `hmm-lush` rules use for `Code/Utility/`.
/segalign/src/segalign/*
!/segalign/src/segalign/__init__.py
!/segalign/src/segalign/seq/
!/segalign/src/segalign/glob/
!/segalign/src/segalign/data/

# The merge target: dataset.py (the container and its encoder), alignment.py (the
# consumer -- `dataseq`'s shape is only legible against what reads it).
/segalign/src/segalign/seq/*
!/segalign/src/segalign/seq/*.py

# `glob/__init__.py` imports `needleman_wunsch`, which is why this is here rather
# than in the alignment-scope set turned away below. Note that it is also the one
# file in this tree that differs from `ca97809` -- the working copy was modified
# and not committed, so a claim checked against GitHub will not match it.
/segalign/src/segalign/glob/*
!/segalign/src/segalign/glob/*.py

# --- the corpus: All.csv only ------------------------------------------------
# `Dataset.from_directories` walks this tree for `All.csv` (tab-separated, columns
# Text/Stress/Boundary/Pitch/Token) and builds the vocabulary from one of them,
# so these files are both the input-format specification and what makes the tests
# below executable rather than merely readable. 50 files, 200 KB.
#
# Contrast the `.sds` directories in `.scratch/hmm-lush/`, which had to be tracked
# whole because `_size` is a count the loader trusts, so a partial copy is corrupt
# rather than small. There is no such count here: `from_directories` globs the
# directories it finds and skips a verse whose `All.csv` is absent. Tracking a
# subset would therefore be safe -- all 50 are kept only because they are cheap.
#
# The `**/` negation re-includes directories so git will descend; the root
# `.gitignore` is what stops that from reaching `__pycache__/`.
/segalign/src/segalign/data/**/*
!/segalign/src/segalign/data/**/
!/segalign/src/segalign/data/**/__init__.py
!/segalign/src/segalign/data/**/All.csv

# --- tests: the seq/ suite ---------------------------------------------------
# Tests are evidence of intended behaviour, and they are the only place the
# encoder's contract is written down as an assertion rather than a docstring.
# They exercise the real corpus above via `from_directories("chant/greg/tract/m8")`
# rather than fixtures, which is why the corpus is tracked at all.
/segalign/tests/*
!/segalign/tests/__init__.py
!/segalign/tests/seq/
/segalign/tests/seq/*
!/segalign/tests/seq/*.py

# =============================================================================
# SegAlign-Draft -- evidence only
# =============================================================================
# `9dc37b9` (2025-06-30), the predecessor. It is NOT a fourth `dataseq`
# implementation: it has no sequence abstraction at all, and that absence is the
# finding goal 4 records. An absence is a negative claim, which a directory
# listing evidences about as well as a file does -- so one signature is tracked
# to make it positive, and nothing else.
#
# `ss2_alignment(seq1: List[Any], seq2: List[Any], ...)` is that signature:
# sequences enter the alignment as bare Python lists, with no container, no
# vocabulary and no encoder anywhere in the tree.

/SegAlign-Draft/*
!/SegAlign-Draft/README.md
!/SegAlign-Draft/requirements.txt
!/SegAlign-Draft/src/
/SegAlign-Draft/src/*
!/SegAlign-Draft/src/segalign/
/SegAlign-Draft/src/segalign/*
!/SegAlign-Draft/src/segalign/glob/
/SegAlign-Draft/src/segalign/glob/*
!/SegAlign-Draft/src/segalign/glob/ss2_alignment.py

# --- deliberately not tracked, with reasons ----------------------------------
# Named here rather than left to the catch-all so that the reasoning survives.
#
# The bar is higher for this import than for the other two, and the reason is
# provenance. `hmm-lush` was under no version control and was irreplaceable, so
# tracking it was preservation. Both trees here are clean checkouts of live
# GitHub repositories at recorded revisions, so anything turned away below costs
# one `git clone` to recover -- and since `.scratch/` is deleted by the last goal
# of this branch, tracking alignment code "for the `align` package later" would
# not survive to reach `align` in any case.
#
#   SegAlign-Draft/SegAlign.env/   927 MB virtualenv.
#   segalign/.venv/                460 MB.
#   */.git-disabled/               165 MB of history, still readable with
#                                  `git --git-dir=<path>/.git-disabled log`.
#   SegAlign-Draft/src/segalign/tcoffee/
#                                  6 modules, ~1000 lines: a T-Coffee multiple
#                                  alignment implementation with guide tree and
#                                  extended library. Substantial, and squarely in
#                                  `pfsmgraph-align`'s scope rather than
#                                  `dataseq`'s. Recoverable from 9dc37b9.
#   SegAlign-Draft/src/segalign/{util,input,loc}/, .../glob/edit_cost.py
#                                  Same reasoning -- alignment scope.
#   SegAlign-Draft/{notebooks,Legacy,Data,docker}/
#                                  129 MB, 15 MB, 3.4 MB. Exploratory, superseded,
#                                  and unreferenced by the tracked signature.
#   segalign/notebooks/, main.py, uv.lock
#                                  Exploratory; a lock for a project we are not
#                                  building.
#   segalign/src/segalign/data/**/{Input.py,input.py,input_template.py}
#                                  50 files, 204 KB. The hand-authored sources the
#                                  All.csv files were generated from. Not imported
#                                  (`data/__init__.py` declares `__all__ = []` and
#                                  imports no subpackage), and the CSVs are what
#                                  `from_directories` actually reads.
#   segalign/src/segalign/data/**/*.{png,svg,txt,ipynb}
#                                  Engraved score images and LilyPond output --
#                                  4.4 MB of the 4.6 MB under `data/`.
#   segalign/CLAUDE.md.orig,       Another project's agent instructions and tool
#   segalign/.claude/              configuration. See the header.
